5787353515625, 0.034423828125, -0.028656005859375, -0.029205322265625, -0.0129852294921875, -0.005794525146484375, 0.004978179931640625, 0.01271820068359375, 0.00186920166015625, 0.0006361007690429688, 0.005672454833984375, -0.01497650146484375, -0.0203857421875, 0.039398193359375, 0.004512786865234375, 0.03143310546875, -0.0003867149353027344, 0.01508331298828125, -0.0225372314453125, 0.032989501953125, 0.0028362274169921875, 0.0015125274658203125, 0.0144195556640625, -0.0203094482421875, -0.003490447998046875, 0.00800323486328125, -0.00992584228515625, -0.021453857421875, 0.03564453125, 0.016632080078125, 0.021453857421875, -0.019775390625, 0.00804901123046875, -0.01495361328125, 0.0079345703125, 0.028411865234375, -0.004413604736328125, 0.0078125, -0.001567840576171875, -0.01358795166015625, 0.0073699951171875, -0.04437255859375, -0.02264404296875, 0.010162353515625, -0.012359619140625, 0.021759033203125, -0.00794219970703125, 0.020233154296875, 0.0252227783203125, -0.0024814605712890625, -0.01666259765625, -0.004016876220703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose and Rationale:   \n\nStructured, high-quality radiology reporting is essential for clinical care and auditing; however, reports often contain non-standardized language, anatomical ambiguity, and documentation lapses that can compromise downstream interpretation and quality assurance (QA). Prior approaches\u2014including strictly rule-based sectioning or single-agent large language models\u2014have demonstrated limited scalability, generalizability, or interpretability for complex, multi-organ findings (see Supplementary Table 1). Many existing systems require cloud-based computation or depend heavily on external data, limiting local adaptability and privacy compliance. To address these gaps, we developed and evaluated a locally deployed multi-agent AI system integrating rule-based anatomical parsing, a fine-tuned language model, and deterministic QA logic, aiming to robustly structure free-text reports and flag clinically relevant inconsistencies under local institutional constraints.\n\nMaterials and Methods:  \n\nThis retrospective, exploratory study analyzed 638 CT radiology reports (chest, abdomen, pelvis) dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single academic center in 2023\u20132024. No external datasets were used for model development or validation. Inclusion criteria: scanned in specified years, adult patients (>18 years), dictated by included radiologists, and availability of full digital text and demographic metadata. Exclusion criteria: incomplete reports, pediatric studies, or reports with substantial redactions. The main set was randomly sampled according to these rules. The stratified evaluation subset (n=45) was constructed by random selection within QA-flag strata to ensure adequate representation: 20 section mismatch, 10 gender-anatomy conflict, 10 undocumented communication, and 5 unflagged reports, ensuring each report belonged to only one primary error class for clarity.\n\nMulti-Agent AI System Architecture:  \n\n1. **Rule-Based Section Labeling Agent**\n   - **Input:** Report sentences.\n   - **Operation:** Applied deterministic regular expressions anchored to curated anatomical and pathological lexicons (e.g., \u201clung,\u201d \u201cliver,\u201d \u201ckidney,\u201d \u201cadrenal,\u201d \u201cspleen\u201d), derived from consensus radiology vocabularies and augmented through iterative review of 1,000 local sample reports. Overlapping or compound anatomical terms were assigned section labels using established precedence rules (e.g., \u201cliver\u201d > \u201ckidney\u201d > \u201cadrenal\u201d for \u201clesion in liver and right kidney\u201d). The explicit order and final term list (including >50 anatomical entities, e.g., \u201ccolon,\u201d \u201cpancreas,\u201d \u201cpleura,\u201d \u201cuterus,\u201d summarized in Table S1) were finalized after internal error analysis. Sentences with no matching term remained unassigned (\u201cmiscellaneous\u201d). Example: \u201cHypodense lesion in right kidney and left liver lobe\u201d labeled \u201cliver.\u201d\n   - **Output:** Sentences with anatomical section tags.\n\n2. **Locally Deployed Language Model Agent**\n   - **Input:** Anatomically segmented sentences.\n   - **Operation:** Llama 2 13B (self-hosted) fine-tuned exclusively on 15,000 in-house de-identified CT report sections (no overlap with test set) using standard supervised objectives (learning rate 2e-5, batch size 8, 3 epochs, 10% validation split). Validation set monitored for overfitting (no rising loss in late epochs, see Supplementary Figure S1). Llama 2 13B selected after benchmarking against Llama 2 7B and GPT-3.5 in terms of accuracy and compute tradeoffs (Supplementary Table 2). Model assigned section labels to tokenized input and rejected low-probability section assignments beneath a minimum word-match threshold.\n   - **Output:** Structured, labeled reports.\n\n3. **Deterministic QA Agent**\n   - **Input:** Structured reports plus patient demographics.\n   - **Operation:** Utilized explicit rule sets, summarized below and detailed further in Supplementary Materials:\n     - **Section mismatch:** Identified when an abnormality phrase (e.g., \u201cmass,\u201d \u201cnodule\u201d) present in Findings (\u201cKidney mass noted\u201d) lacked a corresponding Impression mention (\u201cNo mention of kidney mass\u201d), with negation/uncertainty detected using NegEx-based string matching.\n     - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** Flagged if gendered organ terms (\u201cuterus,\u201d \u201cprostate,\u201d \u201covaries,\u201d \u201ctesticle\u201d) appeared in patients with incompatible demographic metadata (verified in EHR extraction).\n     - **Undocumented communication:** Flagged if urgent/critical descriptors (\u201ccritical finding,\u201d \u201curgent,\u201d \u201cSTAT,\u201d \u201ccall made\u201d) appeared without explicit documentation of provider notification (\u201creferring physician notified\u201d).\n   - **Output:** Binary QA flags (0/1 or none) per category. Full representative rule set is in Table S2.\n\nEvaluation:  \n\nReview subset (n=45) sampled as detailed above. Two board-certified radiologists (>5 years experience each in abdominal imaging), with prior orientation to study-specific guideline documents (Table S3: reviewer rubric, defined by consensus after pilot review of 10 test cases), independently and blindly scored each agent output for each of four metrics (fidelity, accuracy, omission, fabrication; definitions in Supplementary Table S4). Reviewers were blinded to the original QA flag, patient meta-data, and the other\u2019s scores. Ambiguous or discrepant ratings prompted mandatory discussion with the goal of achieving consensus (identical ratings for all four metrics); if not, a third abdominal radiologist (>10 years experience, senior faculty) adjudicated. All review and adjudication procedures were protocolized in advance and visually summarized in Figure 1.\n\nRatings for overall QA performance: \u201cexcellent\u201d (no errors, high clarity), \u201cgood\u201d (minor, non-clinically significant labeling errors or minor boundary ambiguity), \u201cfair\u201d (structural deficiencies such as mis-assignment in multi-organ findings or incomplete sectioning without clinical information loss).\n\nStatistical Analysis:  \n\nWith limited, stratified sample, only descriptive statistics are reported. Proportions with 95% CIs (Wilson method) characterize structuring and QA outcomes; CIs for error-free rates are non-inferential due to stratified (not simple random) sampling, which oversamples certain error categories and skews base rates. Inter-reviewer reliability was estimated using unweighted/weighted Cohen\u2019s kappa with three-level QA ratings. No hypothesis testing or p-values are presented, and aggregate accuracy rates should not be generalized to general clinical populations due to spectrum bias induced by stratification.\n\nResults:  \n\nThe multi-agent system successfully restructured the Findings from all 638 reports (22,270 sentences). The QA agent flagged 90/638 reports (14.1%): 80 (12.5%) section mismatches, 5 (0.8%) gender-anatomy conflicts, and 5 (0.8%) undocumented communication (Table 1). Example errors:\n   - Section mismatch: \u201cRenal mass\u201d in Findings, not included in Impression.\n   - Gender-anatomy conflict: \u201cProstate\u201d in female patient report.\n   - Undocumented communication: No notice of \u201curgent pulmonary embolism.\u201d\n\nIn the 45 stratified review cases (Tables 2, Supplementary S3):\n   - 31/45 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were rated correctly restructured (\u201cexcellent\u201d/\u201cgood\u201d).\n   - 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) incorrectly structured; both involved mis-assignment of compound findings (\u201cliver and kidney lesions\u201d labeled under single section due to hierarchy rules detailed in Table S2).\n   - 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) reports had initial reviewer disagreement (mainly between \u201cgood\u201d and \u201cfair\u201d for boundary/label ambiguity).\n   - Cohen\u2019s kappa for correct/incorrect structuring = 0.49; weighted kappa for three-level QA categories = 0.55.\n   - Consensus reached after discussion in 40/45 reports; remaining 5 resolved by adjudicator (all board-certified, at least 5 years experience).\n   - No content omissions or fabricated statements in any case (0%, upper CI <7%).\n   - Final overall QA: \u201cExcellent\u201d 17/45 (38%), \u201cGood\u201d 21/45 (46%), \u201cFair\u201d 7/45 (16%). Common errors included ambiguous multi-organ findings not precisely assignable by the current hierarchy (see Supplement S3).\n\nNo direct external or vendor baseline was available for comparison, as commercial structuring solutions were not active at the institution and no standard open-source baseline exists for the full local QA workflow. However, internal benchmarks for manual (human-only) sectioning in a pilot sample (n=20) yielded comparable \u201cexcellent/good\u201d rates (90% vs 84%, respectively; details in Supplement S4), supporting the face validity of the AI system, while the multi-agent approach offered interpretability in flagged errors.\n\nDiscussion:  \n\nStudy limitations include: (1) Single-institution data and locally trained models introduce risk of overfitting to linguistic patterns not present elsewhere; this could inflate structuring performance (see Supplementary Table S5 for cross-institution phrase error examples). (2) Stratified sampling increases apparent rates of rare errors and limits generalizability; CIs do not support external inference. (3) Reviewer preconceptions\u2014including familiarity with local report phrasing\u2014may bias scoring upward; mitigation included strict blinding and use of a third-party adjudicator, and protocols will be pre-registered in future work to calibrate reviewer variability. (4) Rule-based term lists and hierarchy offer interpretability but are not exhaustive (edge cases may escape deterministic patterns); ongoing list/logic refinement and adversarial testing with externalized \u201cedge\u201d reports is recommended. The multi-agent architecture enables clear error traceability, but complex compound findings (e.g., \u201cliver and kidney mass\u201d in same sentence) remain challenging for hierarchy-based sectioning\u2014this contributed to the two erroneous cases (Table"}}